### Exception Handling Architecture: try, catch, finally, and Multiple Catch

#### 1. Core Principle & Architecture:
In Java, exceptions are objects derived from `java.lang.Throwable`. When an error occurs inside a method, the JVM constructs an exception object and throws it onto the call stack. The structured exception handling mechanism isolates error-handling logic from normal application business logic.

```
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |                 try Block                   |
       |  (Guards critical code prone to exceptions) |
       +---------------------------------------------+
                              |
                     [Exception Thrown]
                              v
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |                catch Block(s)               |
       |     (Specific handlers matched in order)     |
       +---------------------------------------------+
                              |
                     [Always Transition]
                              v
       +---------------------------------------------+
       |                finally Block                |
       |   (Deterministic resource/stream cleanup)   |
       +---------------------------------------------+
```

#### 2. Multiple Catch Clauses & Precedence Rule:
A single `try` block can be followed by multiple `catch` blocks to handle different exceptions specifically.
> **Cardinal Precedence Rule:** Subclass exception types must **always precede** superclass exception types. If a superclass catch (e.g. `catch (Exception e)`) is placed first, subsequent specialized catches become unreachable, resulting in a compile-time error.

#### 3. Complete Demonstrative Java Implementation:
```java
import java.io.*;

public class ExceptionHandlingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("--- Starting Execution ---");

        try {
            int[] numbers = { 10, 20, 30 };
            String text = null;

            // Nested try block
            try {
                System.out.println("Accessing invalid index: " + numbers[5]);
            } catch (ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException aie) {
                System.err.println("Nested Catch: Array index out of range: " + aie.getMessage());
            }

            // Trigger arithmetic error
            int divisor = 0;
            int quotient = 100 / divisor;
            System.out.println("Quotient = " + quotient);

        } catch (ArithmeticException ae) {
            System.err.println("Caught ArithmeticException: Cannot divide integer by zero.");
        } catch (NullPointerException npe) {
            System.err.println("Caught NullPointerException: Attempted operation on null reference.");
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // General fallback handler
            System.err.println("General Exception caught: " + e.getMessage());
        } finally {
            System.out.println("Finally Block Executed: Releasing system locks and resources.");
        }

        System.out.println("--- Program Continues Normal Execution ---");
    }
}
```

#### 4. Traditional Error Codes vs. Java Exception Handling:
| Parameter | Traditional C-Style Error Codes | Java Exception Handling |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Separation of Concerns** | Mixed with normal logic (frequent `if (err != 0)`). | Clean separation via dedicated `catch` blocks. |
| **Error Propagation** | Must be explicitly passed through return values. | Automatically propagated up the call stack until caught. |
| **Type Safety** | Integer return codes lack context or detail. | Rich polymorphic objects containing stack traces and messages. |
| **Cleanup Guarantee** | Easily bypassed if a developer omits return checks. | Guaranteed execution via the `finally` block. |

$$\therefore \text{Java exception handling provides robust, fault-tolerant execution with deterministic cleanup.}$$
